```
Detection: Kerberoasting (RC4 service ticket requests)
MITRE ATT&CK: T1558.003 - Steal or Forge Kerberos Tickets: Kerberoasting
Data source: Windows Security Event Log (index=winlogs)
Origin: gap identified during purple-team exercise (see ../attack-log.md Run 1)

Logic: TGS requests (4769) using RC4 encryption (0x17) are abnormal in a
modern domain (AES should be the default) and are the classic Kerberoasting
signature — an attacker requesting service tickets offline-crackable for
the service account's password.
```

index=winlogs EventCode=4769 Ticket_Encryption_Type=0x17
| stats count by Account_Name, Service_Name, src_ip
| where count > 3

```
False positives:
- Legacy applications/service accounts still configured for RC4 (should be
  remediated, but exists in real environments)
- Domain controllers doing routine ticket renewal for legacy-compatible
  service accounts

Analyst triage:
1. Check if Service_Name corresponds to a known legacy app with a
   documented RC4 requirement — if so, this is a config-hygiene finding,
   not an active attack (still worth a ticket to force AES)
2. If the requesting account is requesting tickets for MANY different
   service accounts in a short window: high-confidence Kerberoasting,
   escalate immediately
3. Recommend remediation: enforce AES-only Kerberos encryption via GPO
   (Network security: Configure encryption types allowed for Kerberos)
```
